WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.540
How do you keep sensitive plot details secret?&nbsp;
Easy: don't give Tom Holland a script. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.160
joking! In fact, he's far from the only&nbsp;
actor who's spoiled a huge blockbuster.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.480
Will Smith has spent enough time in Hollywood&nbsp;
to know that spoiling the ending of a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.220
before it comes out is a surefire way&nbsp;
to ruin it for an audience. But in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.960
he did just that at a news conference while&nbsp;
talking about his film "I Am Legend." As Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.340
began rambling about the film, co-producer and&nbsp;
co-screenwriter Akiva Goldsman realized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.900
he had gone too far, and he yelled, "Don't&nbsp;
give away the ending!" But it was too late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.920
and Smith had already crossed the line.&nbsp;
The journalists present were instructed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.180
not to report on the ending of the film, so&nbsp;
thankfully, they were able to do damage control.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.260
Since "I Am Legend" was based on a novel of&nbsp;
the same name that had already been adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.220
multiple times, Smith might have figured that&nbsp;
being loose-lipped about the details wasn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.420
big deal — after all, plenty of people had&nbsp;
already read the book, so there weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:00.720
really any secrets to keep, right? Well, not&nbsp;
quite. The screenwriters completely changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.380
the ending of the film, so ideally, it was&nbsp;
supposed to be a surprise for everyone.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.300
With a franchise as huge as "Star Wars,"&nbsp;
spoilers are bound to get out every once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.520
in a while. At the 2016 Star Wars Celebration in&nbsp;
London, Jiang Wen, who played Baze Malbus in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.600
then-upcoming "Rogue One: A Star Wars Story,"&nbsp;
answered a question about his own character. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.920
started off by saying that he couldn't say much&nbsp;
about his own fate, but then he proceeded to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.120
tell everyone in attendance that Donnie Yen's&nbsp;
character, Chirrut Imwe, was going to die.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.900
"And when this guy's dead, I do something better."

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.100
Luckily, panel emcee Gwendoline Christie&nbsp;
came to the rescue and managed to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.060
Wen from giving anything else away. While&nbsp;
he did stick to his promise about keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.040
his own character's fate a secret, he&nbsp;
did spoil another one. Whoops! It seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.120
like he was so eager to deflect the attention&nbsp;
from himself that he said a little too much.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.700
In 2009, Rachel McAdams starred in the film&nbsp;
"The Time Traveler's Wife," which was based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.240
on the novel of the same name. McAdams&nbsp;
could perhaps be forgiven for assuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.200
that the target audience for the adaptation&nbsp;
was already familiar with the book. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.400
the plot of any film that involves time&nbsp;
travel will almost definitely include some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.960
trippy twists and turns, and that was the&nbsp;
case with "The Time Traveler's Wife." But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.140
those twists aren't nearly as interesting&nbsp;
when you know what's coming in advance.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.900
McAdams began revealing major plot twists during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.300
a 2009 interview with Jon&nbsp;
Stewart on "The Daily Show."

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.820
"He goes forward as far as his own death."

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:24.420
"Whoa!"

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:30.060
When she realized she had said too much,&nbsp;
she didn't try to backtrack. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.640
it just seemed like she figured&nbsp;
she might as well run with it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.580
keep talking. Stewart joked that he&nbsp;
still couldn't wait to see the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.120
but after watching that interview, you&nbsp;
really wouldn't need to buy a ticket.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.340
"But obviously that's not the plot."

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.140
Gwyneth Paltrow has always had trouble holding&nbsp;
back when it comes to spoilers. Throughout her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.340
time playing Pepper Potts in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe, she has been known to let key details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.080
slip before the premieres. Before the release&nbsp;
of "Avengers: Endgame," she revealed a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.040
detail about Tony Stark and Pepper Potts'&nbsp;
lives after the tragic events of "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.320
Infinity War"... and in doing so, she gave&nbsp;
away a lot more than she probably realized.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.260
In an interview for the official&nbsp;
"Infinity War" magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.860
Paltrow was asked about Pepper's&nbsp;
relationship with Tony. She answered:

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.820
"Pepper and Tony have had a real long&nbsp;
journey together. She obviously starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.460
as his dutiful assistant, and&nbsp;
then the relationship evolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.520
and now this decade later they're&nbsp;
married, and they have a child."

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.460
Endgame, 40:13, Tony puts his&nbsp;
hand over his mouth, "S---!"

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.680
Yeah, that last part was a big spoiler for&nbsp;
fans eagerly awaiting Endgame. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.500
Pepper and Tony were a galaxy apart&nbsp;
following the events of Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.380
Infinity War, and had only briefly&nbsp;
talked about having children.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.560
"So you woke up and thought that we were…"

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.000
"Expecting."

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.480
"Yeah."

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:45.960
"Yes?"

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:46.500
"No."

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.360
Fans had no idea they would be reunited and would&nbsp;
have a child after the time-jump in "Endgame."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.200
What's most surprising is that the official&nbsp;
magazine printed the quote in the first place.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.320
Michael Keaton dropped the mother of all spoilers&nbsp;
while promoting Tim Burton's 1989 "Batman" film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.460
During an interview with David Letterman, the&nbsp;
two started delving into Batman's origin story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:10.080
and his connection with the Joker. In the midst&nbsp;
of their conversation, Keaton seemingly forgot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.520
that the audience was there for a second, because&nbsp;
he got a bit carried away describing the plot.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.380
"I witness…Batman, Bruce Wayne witnesses&nbsp;
the murder of his parents when he's a kid."

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.580
"And the Joker had done that?"

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.340
"Yeah, but he doesn't know it 'til later.&nbsp;
Oh, I'm kind of blowing the plot here."

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.240
This, of course, was not an element&nbsp;
of existing Batman mythology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.000
invented as a plot twist for the '89 film.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.400
Needless to say, this was a shock for both&nbsp;
Letterman and his audience. But by that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.840
the damage had been done, and the audience was&nbsp;
probably a little disappointed. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.180
the spoiler clearly didn't affect the success&nbsp;
of the film, which went on to make $411 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.520
worldwide, revitalize the comic industry, and&nbsp;
help define superhero movies as we know them.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.780
For some reason, it seems exceptionally&nbsp;
difficult for the casts of superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.100
films to keep quiet about key plot points.&nbsp;
Just look at Jason Momoa's slip up on "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.820
Late Late Show with James Corden." While&nbsp;
promoting their new film "Aquaman," Momoa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.120
and his co-star Yahya Abdul-Mateen&nbsp;
II made an appearance on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.600
and they let the audience in on a few&nbsp;
things they weren't really supposed to know.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.700
Momoa started talking about the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.160
between their two characters&nbsp;
and had a slip of the tongue.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.220
"But then there's a whole emotional scene&nbsp;
with his father because I kill his dad. Oop!"

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.500
Momoa went on to joke that this is why he always&nbsp;
has Abdul-Mateen answer the questions. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.700
if he'd been in character as Aquaman, he'd&nbsp;
have been more tightlipped about the plot.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.080
"Something something trident."

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.060
See? That's better!

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.740
The ending of "Avengers: Infinity War" was&nbsp;
gut-wrenching for Marvel fans. When Thanos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.940
snapped his fingers, half of all life&nbsp;
in the universe disappeared. But if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.600
caught a particular "Good Morning America"&nbsp;
interview with Don Cheadle and Mark Ruffalo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.200
that aired almost a year before the movie&nbsp;
came out, you might have seen it coming.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.240
"Wait 'til you see this next one. Everybody dies."

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.480
"Dude, dude, dude."
"Not everybody….no, was that…?"

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.280
It's true that Ruffalo wasn't too specific with&nbsp;
this spoiler, so it didn't seem too memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.600
at the time. But some fans probably did start&nbsp;
to suspect that depressing finale — there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.500
no doubt that some of them fully expected&nbsp;
many of the Avengers to perish. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.320
having some time to steel yourself&nbsp;
emotionally might have made it easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:18.300
to accept your favorite character's demise.&nbsp;
So maybe we should have thanked Mark Ruffalo.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.600
"You're marginally better than&nbsp;
Tom Holland, but that's it."

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.460
"I taught Tom Holland."

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.460
Tom Holland is the undisputed king of Marvel movie&nbsp;
spoilers. The actor has dropped so many spoilers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:34.740
during interviews that at this point, it's become&nbsp;
a running joke. But Holland is so enthusiastic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.520
endearing that it's hard to feel annoyed at&nbsp;
him for his habit of letting spoilers slip.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.160
Before a screening of "Avengers:&nbsp;
Infinity War" in Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.660
Holland made an appearance and shouted: "I'm&nbsp;
alive!" When the audience seemed confused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.040
Holland's mistake slowly dawned on him&nbsp;
— he was under the impression that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.960
had just seen the film and knew his character&nbsp;
would die, but it hadn't started yet. Whoops!

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.760
Jiang Wen might have spoiled the fate of one&nbsp;
character in "Rogue One," but many years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.620
that, David Prowse ruined the biggest plot twist&nbsp;
in "Star Wars" history. When the man behind Darth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.480
Vader's mask was interviewed about "The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back" in 1978 — two years before it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.400
even released — he openly shared one of the most&nbsp;
shocking moments in any of the "Star Wars" films.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.460
Prowse was at a fan meet-up when&nbsp;
he dropped a bomb regarding Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.680
Skywalker and Darth Vader's relationship, saying:

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.920
"Father can't kill son, son can't kill father."

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:28.440
Thankfully, his reveal was only printed in a&nbsp;
single local newspaper article. And clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.240
not many people read the original article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.180
because the vast majority of fans&nbsp;
were still totally caught off guard.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.440
If you don't recognize the name Tyler&nbsp;
Nelson, there's a reason for that:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.640
he put a stop to his acting career when&nbsp;
it had barely begun. Nelson was cast as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.120
extra in "Indiana Jones and the Kingdom&nbsp;
of the Crystal Skull," and naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.680
he was over the moon about the opportunity to be&nbsp;
a part of an "Indiana Jones" film. His role was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.400
apparently to play a Russian dancing after&nbsp;
Indy was captured. His involvement got some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.420
attention in his hometown, and a local newspaper&nbsp;
interviewed him to get the scoop on the film.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:01.080
But instead of simply sticking to&nbsp;
the story of how he ended up on set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.400
he started revealing the entire plot of&nbsp;
the film. He might as well have handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:10.140
the reporter a script. He completely violated&nbsp;
his non-disclosure agreement, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.820
he faced some serious consequences. Once&nbsp;
word got back to the studio that Nelson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.120
had blabbed, Steven Spielberg's&nbsp;
spokesman, Marvin Levy, commented,

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.480
"Who knows whether that particular person&nbsp;
will ever work in this town again?"

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.580
Nelson was promptly cut from the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.820
and his mistake appears to have&nbsp;
cost him a future in acting.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.720
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" features several&nbsp;
returning actors from prior films in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.320
franchise, including Tobey Maguire and&nbsp;
Andrew Garfield as returning Spider-Men.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:35.520
"You okay?"

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.920
"It's my back. It's kind of stiff&nbsp;
from all the swinging I guess."

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.700
While Marvel's attempts at keeping a lid on&nbsp;
the film's biggest comebacks were always going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.140
to be tough considering how many people were&nbsp;
involved, Alfred Molina did them no favors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:53.160
The returning Doctor Octopus actor spilled the&nbsp;
beans more than a year before the film's release.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.660
The rumors started when Molina was spotted on the&nbsp;
set of the production, and the question came up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.500
as to his involvement during an interview with&nbsp;
Variety. Assuming that published reports of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.400
appearance had already made it official,&nbsp;
the actor wasn't shy about it. He said:

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.640
"When we were shooting it, we were&nbsp;
all under orders not to talk about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.000
because it was supposed to be some&nbsp;
great big secret. But, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.320
it's all over the internet. I actually described&nbsp;
myself as the worst kept secret in Hollywood!"

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.860
"Up here. He's up there. It's&nbsp;
the guy from the bridge."

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.340
A year after the film's release,&nbsp;
though, Molina told the Radio Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.580
that the studio was pretty miffed that&nbsp;
he'd confirmed his appearance. He said:

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.120
"Kevin Feige was on a red carpet somewhere&nbsp;
and someone asked him about some Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.420
movie and apparently he got a bit&nbsp;
snippy and turned around and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.540
'Ask Alfred Molina.' So I got&nbsp;
into a little bit of hot water."

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.740
The "Star Wars" prequel trilogy was&nbsp;
among the first major movie series to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.480
be produced in the era of internet spoilers. As&nbsp;
fan communities popped up in the mid-to-late '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.440
it became harder and harder to prevent plot&nbsp;
leaks from reaching the masses. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.280
Lucasfilm surely took security seriously&nbsp;
to prevent spoilers, they couldn't stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.660
one of their own stars — Samuel L. Jackson&nbsp;
— from giving away his own character's fate.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.320
Jackson played the lightsaber-wielding&nbsp;
warrior Mace Windu in the trilogy. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.100
was also one of the biggest actors involved in&nbsp;
the trilogy and was always being asked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:09.660
his part in the films. And in one particularly&nbsp;
revealing interview with Now Playing Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.200
he talked a little bit too much when&nbsp;
asked about what part his character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.200
had to play in the third prequel.&nbsp;
Jackson bluntly told the magazine:

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.880
"Dying! Yeah, like all of the rest of the&nbsp;
Jedi. I can't tell you [the specifics],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.380
but yeah, it's an awesome death."

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.240
"So the prophecy says."

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.720
Admittedly, most fans probably&nbsp;
knew this was likely given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.540
backstory established in earlier&nbsp;
films, but it was a startling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:34.260
admission from an otherwise tight-lipped&nbsp;
production and sent fans into a frenzy.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.120
When your big blockbuster movie is based on a&nbsp;
well-loved and widely read comic book universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.660
it might be hard to keep all of&nbsp;
the story a secret. The movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.420
biggest plot points might be common&nbsp;
knowledge among comic book readers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.260
but that doesn't mean wider audiences know&nbsp;
what's coming. Such was the case for "X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:54.060
Apocalypse," the third film in the relaunched&nbsp;
franchise. Fan favorite Peter Maximoff — aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:58.620
Quicksilver — returns from the previous film, and&nbsp;
one of the major twists is the revelation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:03.240
he's the son of longtime "X-Men" villain Magneto,&nbsp;
a fact that comic fans might've already guessed.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:04.800
"Are you going to tell him?"

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.540
"Might. One day."

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.380
Unfortunately, actor Evan Peters just&nbsp;
couldn't keep the secret for everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:16.740
else while talking to JoBlo about his&nbsp;
character's place in the film. He said:

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.560
"I have learned that [Magneto's]&nbsp;
my father at this point. [...] He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.560
knows who he is now so he is trying&nbsp;
to find him. He's been searching for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.560
him. It's been 10 years and he hasn't&nbsp;
found him and then something happens."

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.920
Ultimately, of course, "X-Men:&nbsp;
Apocalypse" wasn't the popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:32.820
acclaimed blockbuster that the previous&nbsp;
films had been, and the spoiled revelation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.720
wasn't something that wound up ruining the&nbsp;
movie. Thankfully, though, Peters remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.560
much more tight-lipped when it came to his&nbsp;
return in Marvel's "WandaVision" in 2021.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:49.320
You might not expect a modest, low-budget&nbsp;
character drama about a man and his dog to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:53.460
be the subject of a major spoiler discussion,&nbsp;
but here we are. Starring Channing Tatum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:58.080
the 2022 film "Dog" was inspired by the actor's&nbsp;
own life and a road trip he took with his dying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.460
dog in 2018. The film doesn't include any truly&nbsp;
dramatic plot twists, stunning revelations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.020
or major mind-benders. In fact, it's a&nbsp;
fairly light-hearted, family-friendly tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:11.460
with a happy ending. But that's exactly why Tatum&nbsp;
himself gave away the ending before it released.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:15.900
In a kind of "reverse spoiler," Tatum talked&nbsp;
about what didn't happen in the movie, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:20.340
wasn't a mistake or slip-up. In fact, it was in&nbsp;
an attempt to attract more people to the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.840
considering the history of animal movies like&nbsp;
"The Call of the Wild" and "Marley &amp; Me" — both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.800
were advertised as upbeat family films&nbsp;
and end as tearjerkers. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.300
Tatum wanted to assure audiences that "Dog" would&nbsp;
not conclude with a depressing or morose ending.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:39.660
"It's a good ending, so everyone&nbsp;
knows. See, everybody's like, "Huhh.'"

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.620
When it comes to tragic character deaths,&nbsp;
few can top the emotional tour de force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:46.620
that audiences endure in "Logan,"&nbsp;
the third solo film for Wolverine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.320
played by Hugh Jackman. His&nbsp;
ninth appearance in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:53.460
it was highly publicized as the last time&nbsp;
Jackman would wield the adamantium claws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.760
and the rumor mill was spinning that Logan&nbsp;
wouldn't survive the events of the movie.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:01.260
Very loosely adapted from the epic comic&nbsp;
book miniseries "Old Man Logan," the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:05.460
might not have killed off its hero had it stuck&nbsp;
closer to its source material, but in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:09.960
with Jackman insistent on retiring the role, it&nbsp;
indeed ends with the death of the title character.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.020
"So, this is what it feels like."

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:18.060
But while many were expecting Logan's demise, it&nbsp;
most assuredly was not a certainty going into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:22.560
release of the film. That is, not until Jackman&nbsp;
himself decided to say it rather plainly in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.100
interview with Entertainment Weekly. The interview&nbsp;
was published the day after the film's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:30.420
but for everyone who couldn't make it to the&nbsp;
premiere, it was a huge spoiler. Jackman told EW:

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:35.160
"As soon as I saw the script, I got it. Logan&nbsp;
is someone [who’s] most scared of intimacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.880
and so he wants to be alone and do things&nbsp;
for himself. The idea that, in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.240
he must give his life to save someone&nbsp;
else... I thought that was really powerful."

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:47.100
While his casting was announced all the&nbsp;
way back in 2007, Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.060
finally starred as the titular anti-hero in&nbsp;
"Black Adam" when it was released in 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.380
Though it lacked Shazam — the hero whose comic&nbsp;
books originally spawned Black Adam back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.380 --> 00:14:00.420
1940s — the film still has plenty of surprises.&nbsp;
This includes the Justice Society of America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:05.700
an iconic superhero team featuring heroes like&nbsp;
Doctor Fate and Hawkman. But it also included a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:10.200
jaw-dropping post-credits scene that had to be&nbsp;
seen to be believed, as Henry Cavill reprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:14.880
his role as the Man of Steel in a sequence that&nbsp;
set up a future clash of titans. The only problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.600
was The Rock himself had already spoiled the&nbsp;
scene in the days leading up to the film.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.260
While promoting the movie on the red carpet&nbsp;
of the film's world premiere — more than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.920
week before its wide release — Johnson talked&nbsp;
to Entertainment Weekly and decided to let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.740
the cat out of the bag when asked about&nbsp;
the presence of Superman in the film.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:33.780
"We've all been fighting for this moment,&nbsp;
so I will say this: Welcome home."

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:37.980
Though he didn't say it explicitly, Entertainment&nbsp;
Weekly knew exactly what he was saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.520
and tweeted out the clip with the announcement&nbsp;
that Henry Cavill had returned as Superman and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.360
was back in the DCEU. As we all know&nbsp;
now, of course, that didn't last.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.160
"Star Wars: Revenge of the Sith" wasn't the last&nbsp;
major blockbuster movie that Samuel L. Jackson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:54.780
wound up spoiling in his effort to promote his&nbsp;
next big flick. Nearly 15 years later in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.780 --> 00:15:00.480
Jackson starred in "Captain Marvel," a prequel&nbsp;
to the MCU set in the 1990s. In addition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:04.860
introducing cosmic hero Carol Danvers, it shed&nbsp;
new light on the history of Jackson's Nick Fury.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:07.920
Digitally de-aged, Jackson&nbsp;
was a major part of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:11.340
which revealed Fury's first meeting with&nbsp;
a superhero and how he conceived of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:15.060
Avengers Initiative. But that wasn't all, and&nbsp;
one of the most fun revelations was spoiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:19.380
by Jackson himself — across two separate&nbsp;
interviews — before the movie even released.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:23.100
It all started while Jackson was speaking&nbsp;
with Entertainment Tonight in January of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:27.420
2019. He recklessly revealed that the film would&nbsp;
tell the story behind Fury's famous eyepatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:31.320
but didn't elaborate on how he'd lose&nbsp;
it. That detail would come while he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.620
being interviewed for The Playlist just&nbsp;
days before the film landed in theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.080
though it may not have been entirely his fault.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.380
Having seen the film ahead of its&nbsp;
official release, the interviewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:44.100
directly addressed how he lost the eye. Well,&nbsp;
Jackson went right along and talked about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:48.000
even addressing rumors that he doesn't&nbsp;
like cats, an allegation he denied.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:52.920
"I'm gonna give him all the&nbsp;
love and hugs that he need."

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:57.780
At the time of its rollout, one of the biggest&nbsp;
stars in Phase 1 of the MCU was Natalie Portman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:02.340
in 2011's "Thor." The A-list actor had just won&nbsp;
an Oscar earlier that year and starred opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:06.240
Chris Hemsworth as Jane Foster, a scientist&nbsp;
who discovers the Asgardian prince after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.900
he's banished to Earth. But following&nbsp;
a bad experience on the sequel, "Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:14.340
The Dark World," Portman swore off working for&nbsp;
Marvel. Some years later, the prospect of working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:18.600
with the director Taika Waititi was enough to lure&nbsp;
her back to the MCU for "Thor: Love &amp; Thunder."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.560
But in her excitement, she also spoiled a&nbsp;
major plot twist before the movie came out.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:26.400
While it was well known thanks to trailers&nbsp;
that her character Jane Foster would be given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:29.880
the powers of Thor, what audiences who&nbsp;
weren't comics readers probably didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.320
see coming was that Foster is stricken with&nbsp;
terminal cancer, and her powers allow her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:39.240
stave off her own death. Unfortunately, Portman&nbsp;
went a little too far in talking about all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:42.300
while speaking with Hindustan Times while&nbsp;
promoting the film a month early. She said:

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:46.680
"She's new at it. And also keeps reverting&nbsp;
to human form, so there's kind of danger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.100
always that, like, it might be her last&nbsp;
moment getting to experience that."

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:52.680
But apparently Portman realized her snafu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.040
because she immediately acknowledged the&nbsp;
cardinal sin she'd just committed and added:

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:01.260
"I don't know, that might be...&nbsp;
that might be a big spoiler, sorry."

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:05.460
When it was announced that Kang the Conqueror&nbsp;
would be the villain of the third "Ant-Man" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:09.960
speculation went into overdrive on what the&nbsp;
film might be about. Paul Rudd remained cagey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:14.160
on the details, but his co-star Evangeline&nbsp;
Lilly let the excitement of her return get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.520
the better of her. Eager to share her prep work&nbsp;
on the film ahead of the movie's production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:21.780
the actor took to social media, posting&nbsp;
images for her legions of followers. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.960
they briefly saw was the front cover&nbsp;
of the film's screenplay, and while it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:28.860
wasn't enough to reveal any of the script's&nbsp;
secrets, who she tagged gave the game away.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:34.200
Because in addition to tagging her known co-stars,&nbsp;
she also curiously tagged Corey Stoll. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.680
who had co-starred in the first film in the&nbsp;
series as the villain Yellowjacket had not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:41.520
been officially announced as a member of the&nbsp;
cast, and when the media got wind of her post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:46.440
the rumor mill immediately fired up. Her rushed&nbsp;
deletion of the post only added fuel to the fire.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:50.820
As many suspected, Stoll did wind up&nbsp;
returning in the threequel. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.060
though, Lilly never let slip that his&nbsp;
character had been transformed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:58.200
the comic book villain MODOK, a secret that the&nbsp;
studio was clearly saving for the film's release.

